+      <noscript><div class="notice warn">JavaScript is disabled. The guide and example commands remain readable. Copy buttons and the command builder need JavaScript; select and copy the examples manually.</div></noscript>
+
+      <div class="notice good"><strong>Quick start for file uploads</strong>Send <code>/templates</code>, edit an example and attach it as a document. Put <code>/validate</code> in the file caption to check structure and values only. An empty caption submits valid experiments automatically. <a href="#files">Read the full walkthrough →</a></div>
+
+      <section class="chapter" id="stage-scope"><h2>Current scope: operator-refactored and stage</h2><p>Recorded benchmarks use an exact stage brain image/source, policy UID and frozen snapshot hashes. The default policy is <code>phorvex-staging-demo/demo-api-defense</code>. Select the actual pinned stage calibration policy when it differs. Legacy production/vllm data is not accepted.</p><p><code>/param</code> runs locally against this stage pin. <code>/bench</code> tests and compares the actual base and candidate brain code. A recorded V2/V3 request uses the reviewed typed brain study engine, with exact image-control and local-base checks before a cross-version comparison. Synthetic and Cost/History reference studies retain their separate labels.</p><p>For endpoint studies, the requested SLO window W and history lookback L are independent; each must fit the retained continuous history. For example, a 300-second lookback uses <code>"config": {"lookback": 300}</code>. This does not satisfy a requested 30-day history. The normalizer must observe the full requested SLO/history window; missing actual cost/effects remain null. See the stage operations README and ENDPOINT-V3 guide shipped with the source.</p></section>
+<section class="chapter" id="overview" aria-labelledby="overview-title">
+<div class="chapter-head"><span class="number">01</span><div><h2 id="overview-title">What does the bot do?</h2><p>Parameter sweeps, bounded code changes, and configured Cost/History comparisons.</p></div></div>
+<p>The bot has three workflows: <code>/param</code> compares parameter values using the deployed brain; <code>/bench</code> generates a bounded code change and replays it on recorded states; a <code>study</code> file compares reference Cost/History algorithms with explicit arms and seeds.</p>
+<div class="notice"><strong>Identify the experiment type first.</strong>Replay evaluates decisions on past snapshots. A study compares registered reference algorithms on explicit recorded or synthetic inputs. Neither alone establishes live defense rates, realized cost or verified coverage for counterfactual decisions.</div>
+<p>The bot does not apply these changes to the live brain. Retain the report, configuration and evidence type together. For Cost and History, start with the <a href="#study">dynamic-study walkthrough</a>.</p>
+</section>
+
+      <section class="chapter" id="start" aria-labelledby="start-title">
+        <div class="chapter-head"><span class="number">02</span><div><h2 id="start-title">Access and readiness</h2><p>Set up access once, then run /check before an important experiment.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">1</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Open a private chat with the bot</h3><p><a href="https://t.me/phorvexbot" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open @phorvexbot</a> and press <b>Start</b>. If your account is not authorized yet, ask the bot owner for a fresh invitation, open it, and press Start. The owner can use <code>/invite</code> to create a one-use membership link valid for 24 hours.</p><p class="small muted">Opening the public bot link does not grant membership. The bot does not reply to unauthorized accounts.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">2</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Get help, check dependencies, and download the playbook</h3><p>Send /help and /check as separate messages. Use /guide whenever you want both English guide files attached in this chat:</p><div class="command"><div class="command-top"><span>First message · bot usage</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="cmd-help">Copy</button></div><pre><code id="cmd-help">/help</code></pre></div><div class="command"><div class="command-top"><span>Second message · readiness check</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="cmd-check">Copy</button></div><pre><code id="cmd-check">/check</code></pre></div><div class="command"><div class="command-top"><span>Download this English guide as HTML and PDF</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="cmd-guide">Copy</button></div><pre><code id="cmd-guide">/guide</code></pre></div></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">3</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Check the requirements for your workflow</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Row in /check</th><th>Practical meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Telegram / allowlist / state</td><td>The bot, your authorization, and its job storage must be ready.</td></tr><tr><td>Stage replay runtime / stage brain-replay</td><td>The verified stage pin and replay identity are needed, along with ticks from the same source and policy UID.</td></tr><tr><td>Operator repository</td><td><code>/bench</code> needs the exact baseline brain commit in the server repository.</td></tr><tr><td>Free disk / Go / OpenAI</td><td>Needed to generate code, run tests and build a candidate. The default <code>/bench</code> free-disk threshold is 4 GiB.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="small muted">A <code>WARN</code> can disable only one workflow. Read the row to see what is unavailable. An all-PASS readiness check does not guarantee that future tests or experiments will succeed.</p></div></div>
+      <div class="notice good"><strong>Study prerequisites</strong>An inline-trace study needs no LLM, Go build, source repository or dashboard. A recorded study requires a separately prepared dataset; /check does not check whether that dataset exists.</div></section>
+
+      <section class="chapter" id="choose" aria-labelledby="choose-title">
+<div class="chapter-head"><span class="number">03</span><div><h2 id="choose-title">Choose your workflow</h2><p>Three workflows with different inputs and evidence.</p></div></div>
+<div class="grid3"><article class="card"><span class="badge">study / JSON</span><h3>Compare Cost and History</h3><p>Configure arms, seeds and registered algorithms in a file. No LLM code generation.</p><p><a href="#study">Walkthrough →</a></p></article><article class="card"><span class="badge">/param</span><h3>Vary an existing parameter</h3><p>Use the deployed replay binary’s registered overrides.</p><p><a href="#param">Walkthrough →</a></p></article><article class="card"><span class="badge">/bench</span><h3>Make a bounded code change</h3><p>Specify the file/function; generate a patch, run tests and replay it.</p><p><a href="#bench">Walkthrough →</a></p></article></div><div class="notice">A History proposal, Cost Word document or long experiment description is not automatically an executable request. Use the format for your selected workflow.</div><h3 class="spaced">Options for /param and /bench</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Important detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>policy=phorvex-staging-demo/demo-api-defense</code></td><td>Choose a namespace and policy</td><td>Use <code>namespace/name</code>. This is the current default policy.</td></tr><tr><td><code>ticks=64</code></td><td>Request up to 64 samples for an initial check</td><td>Current default: 500. Allowed request: 1–5000. Actual coverage depends on available, eligible data.</td></tr><tr><td><code>nosync</code></td><td>Use the data and replay identity already on the server</td><td>Without this flag, the bot syncs first. After a new deployment, omit it to refresh the identity and data.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
+        <div class="notice warn"><strong>With nosync, “current” means the last identity cached on the server.</strong>The cache may lag behind a recent live deployment. Check the baseline commit in the report. Sync does not guarantee that the matching source commit exists in the operator repository; missing source must be fetched separately.</div>
+
+</section>
+
+      <section class="chapter" id="param" aria-labelledby="param-title">
+        <div class="chapter-head"><span class="number">04</span><div><h2 id="param-title">Change parameters with /param</h2><p>Explore a supported override without editing code.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">1</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Choose the parameter and hypothesis</h3><p>Example: “If I change the allowed cost ceiling, how do selected moves and estimated disruption cost change?” <code>P-FEA-006</code>, the <b>CHi cost ceiling</b>, is relevant to that question.</p><p class="small muted">The ceiling is in normalized disruption units, not dollars. A lower value makes the cost gate stricter; a higher value makes it more permissive. This relationship alone does not establish better defense.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">2</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Run a small comparison</h3><div class="command"><div class="command-top"><span>Example experiment · up to 64 cached ticks</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="cmd-param-small">Copy</button></div><pre><code id="cmd-param-small">/param P-FEA-006 1.0 1.2 1.5 policy=phorvex-staging-demo/demo-api-defense ticks=64 nosync</code></pre></div><p>The bot automatically adds a control arm using the <strong>recorded effective value</strong> and replays each value you supplied as another arm. You do not need to enter the control value. Do not assume that 1.2 is the current value without checking the report.</p><p class="small muted">Use ASCII digits and a decimal point. Each command accepts 1–11 experiment values. All numbers in this guide are examples, not recommendations for configuring the live brain.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">3</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Read the report, then increase the sample</h3><p>Check status, actual sample size, and each value against the control. If the data and result are interpretable, try a larger sample. This example also syncs fresh data:</p><div class="command"><div class="command-top"><span>Larger sample with a fresh sync</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="cmd-param-large">Copy</button></div><pre><code id="cmd-param-large">/param P-FEA-006 1.0 1.2 1.5 policy=phorvex-staging-demo/demo-api-defense ticks=500</code></pre></div><p class="small muted">The sample or baseline revision may change between runs. Read every experiment against its own control. Check the cohort and commit before comparing numbers from separate reports.</p></div></div>
+        <h3 class="spaced">Known parameters and their limits</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Meaning and unit</th><th>Domain and limitations</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>P-FEA-006</code></td><td>CHi ceiling; normalized disruption</td><td>Greater than 0, up to 1000. No <code>unset</code> override.</td></tr><tr><td><code>P-FEA-005</code></td><td>Proactive hygiene cadence; seconds</td><td>Whole seconds from 1 to 604800. The override changes both cadence and exposure-pressure scale. Fresh trusted event evidence can bypass the cadence gate.</td></tr><tr><td><code>P-OBJ-003</code></td><td>Fixed disruption aversion, or beta</td><td>0–10 and <code>unset</code>. Incompatible with <code>costPreferences</code>; preflight can return <code>MODE_CONFLICT</code>.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
+        <div class="command"><div class="command-top"><span>Second example · 5, 15 and 30 minutes, expressed in seconds</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="cmd-param-cadence">Copy</button></div><pre><code id="cmd-param-cadence">/param P-FEA-005 300 900 1800 policy=phorvex-staging-demo/demo-api-defense ticks=64 nosync</code></pre></div><p class="small muted">An ID in the registry does not mean every parameter can be overridden. The replay binary must support it and preflight must accept it. This cadence example is not a uniform throttle on every attack response.</p>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter" id="bench" aria-labelledby="bench-title">
+        <div class="chapter-head"><span class="number">05</span><div><h2 id="bench-title">Change code and formulas with /bench</h2><p>Turn a clear hypothesis into a patch, tests, and a three-arm comparison.</p></div></div><div class="notice"><strong>Use study JSON for the registered Cost/History experiments.</strong>This chapter is about changing brain code. Its 20% norm-multiplier example does not implement the endpoint-second model or History v1.1. <a href="#study">Study walkthrough</a></div>
+        <div class="notice"><strong>/bench takes a description of a change.</strong>In the current interface, you describe what to change and a model generates a diff. There is no direct branch or commit selector, uploaded-diff execution, or <code>/rerun</code> command.</div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">1</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Define one question you can evaluate</h3><p>A useful example: “If I increase the norm term's weight in the cost penalty by 20%, does estimated disruption cost decrease? What happens to selected moves, predicted coverage, and no-op under attack?”</p><p class="small muted">“Make the brain better” leaves the scope and evaluation unclear. Limit your first experiment to one function or condition. If you do not know the file or function, locate it in the source for the baseline revision first.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">2</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Include these five parts in the request</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Cost-penalty example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Location</td><td><code>phorvex/internal/model/economics.go</code>, function <code>EconomicsMetadata.CostPenalty</code></td></tr><tr><td>Mode</td><td>Only <code>independent-cost-preferences-v1</code></td></tr><tr><td>New formula or behavior</td><td>Change the norm multiplier from 1 to 1.2; keep the flow term as it is.</td></tr><tr><td>Constraints</td><td>Preserve legacy and fixed-beta behavior, feasibility gates, SLO checks, and budgets.</td></tr><tr><td>Change size</td><td>Keep the patch minimal, without unrelated edits or broad refactoring.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="small muted">Use exact file paths, function names and code identifiers. The technical English examples help retrieve relevant context. A fully Persian request without code identifiers may retrieve too little useful context. Verify the example's path and formula against your baseline commit.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">3</span><div class="step-body"><h3>State the old and new formulas explicitly</h3><p>This is an example of defining an experiment. The suitability of 1.2 and whether the change passes existing tests have not been established.</p><div class="formula"><p>Before · only in independent-cost-preferences-v1 mode</p><pre>penalty = w_norm * norm + w_flow * flow</pre></div><div class="formula"><p>After · increase the norm contribution to the penalty by 20%</p><pre>penalty = 1.2 * w_norm * norm + w_flow * flow</pre></div><p class="small muted">For arithmetic illustration only: with <code>w_norm=0.5</code>, <code>norm=2</code>, <code>w_flow=0.3</code> and <code>flow=1</code>, the penalty increases from 1.3 to 1.5. This describes the expression itself. The brain's move count or final estimated cost will not necessarily change by 20%.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">4</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Send the complete command as one message</h3><div class="command"><div class="command-top"><span>Formula-change example · copyable experiment</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="cmd-bench-formula">Copy</button></div><pre><code id="cmd-bench-formula">/bench policy=phorvex-staging-demo/demo-api-defense ticks=64 nosync In phorvex/internal/model/economics.go, change EconomicsMetadata.CostPenalty in independent-cost-preferences-v1 mode from m.NormPreference.Value*norm + m.FlowPreference.Value*flow to 1.2*m.NormPreference.Value*norm + m.FlowPreference.Value*flow. Preserve legacy and fixed-beta behavior, feasibility gates, SLO checks and budgets. Keep the patch minimal.</code></pre></div><div class="notice warn"><strong>Put options before the change description.</strong>The order is <code>/bench</code>, then <code>policy=...</code>, <code>ticks=...</code> and optionally <code>nosync</code>, followed by the description. Options at the end can be interpreted as part of the model request instead.</div><p>To refresh data and the replay identity before the experiment, omit <code>nosync</code>. Use the <a href="#builder">command builder</a> to change the sample size or other options.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">5</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Follow the execution stages</h3><p>The bot gives you a job ID and reports progress. One job runs at a time; up to five can wait in the queue. Cold builds and tests can take several minutes. There is no fixed completion time.</p><div class="command"><div class="command-top"><span>Check the current job and queue</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="cmd-status">Copy</button></div><pre><code id="cmd-status">/status</code></pre></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What the bot does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Baseline and data selection</td><td>Checks replay identity and SHA256, selects ticks from the matching commit, and freezes the selected data for this experiment.</td></tr><tr><td>Patch generation</td><td>The model proposes a change against the baseline commit. The candidate is created in a temporary clone.</td></tr><tr><td>Tests</td><td>Runs Go tests on both versions: <code>internal/decision</code>, <code>cmd/brain-replay</code>, and changed packages. This is not the full repository CI suite.</td></tr><tr><td>Build</td><td>Builds baseline and candidate replays. Both use the same compiler release, read from the deployed binary's build metadata.</td></tr><tr><td>Replay and reporting</td><td>Replays three arms on the same ticks, then sends HTML, CSV, the diff and test results.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="notice danger"><strong>A failed test stops the job before the numerical benchmark.</strong>If baseline or candidate tests fail, the bot sends the corresponding log. An intentional formula change can conflict with an existing numerical contract. Do not remove or bypass tests merely to get a benchmark result.</div></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">6</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Keep all four outputs together</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>File</th><th>What to inspect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>report-&lt;job&gt;.html</code></td><td>Status, commits, data coverage, fidelity, and comparison metrics</td></tr><tr><td><code>metrics-&lt;job&gt;.csv</code></td><td>Values, populations, intervals, data availability, and comparison type</td></tr><tr><td><code>candidate-&lt;job&gt;.diff</code></td><td>Whether the generated code implements exactly what you requested</td></tr><tr><td><code>tests-&lt;job&gt;.json</code></td><td>Baseline and candidate tests, packages, result counts, and toolchain release</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="small muted">File names include the job ID. Read the diff before interpreting the numbers. A successful build does not establish that the model understood your request correctly.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="notice warn"><strong>Every /bench request starts independently.</strong>A second command does not continue the first candidate. It creates a fresh candidate from the baseline commit. Resending the same description with <code>ticks=500</code> does not guarantee the same patch. Compare the diffs. Replaying a fixed candidate deterministically requires a separate engineering workflow.</div>
+        <h3 class="spaced">First run: check the pipeline without changing behavior</h3><p class="small muted">To exercise patch generation, tests, builds and report delivery, request a comment-only change. This is a pipeline check, not an optimization. Conceptually, zero changed decisions is the expected result.</p><div class="command"><div class="command-top"><span>Pipeline check · add a comment only</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="cmd-bench-smoke">Copy</button></div><pre><code id="cmd-bench-smoke">/bench policy=phorvex-staging-demo/demo-api-defense ticks=64 nosync In phorvex/internal/decision/ablation.go, add one explanatory comment immediately before the existing if base.Ratio &gt; 0 branch, explaining that the positive-ratio check avoids division by zero. Change comments only. Preserve all executable code and behavior.</code></pre></div><p class="small muted">Allowed candidate scope: Go files under <code>phorvex/</code>, excluding <code>phorvex/tools/</code>. New <code>_test.go</code> files may be added; existing tests cannot be changed, deleted or renamed. Dependency, <code>go.mod</code>, chart and Dockerfile changes, and file deletions are not accepted by this workflow.</p>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter" id="files" aria-labelledby="files-title">
+        <div class="chapter-head"><span class="number">06</span><div><h2 id="files-title">Run experiments from a file</h2><p>Upload Markdown, TXT or Word with bench, params, or both sections.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">1</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Download a completed example</h3><p>This command sends eight files: five code/parameter examples and three Cost/History JSON studies. This chapter covers the code/parameter formats; <a href="#study">study JSON</a> is separate.</p><div class="command"><div class="command-top"><span>Download completed file examples</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="cmd-templates">Copy</button></div><pre><code id="cmd-templates">/templates</code></pre></div><p class="small muted">Use .md, .txt or .docx. Export Google Docs as Word (.docx) or plain text. Legacy binary .doc is not supported. Maximum document size: 2 MiB; extracted prose: 256 KiB. Study JSON may use the full 2 MiB.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">2</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Fill the sections you want to run</h3><p>A file with both sections produces independent experiments in file order. The comment-only bench example below checks the pipeline; the parameter experiment compares trial ceilings. All values are illustrative.</p><div class="command"><div class="command-top"><span>Case A · both-sections.md or the equivalent Word paragraphs</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="file-example-both" data-copy-kind="file">Copy file text</button></div><pre><code id="file-example-both"># Independent brain experiments
+
+## Bench
+policy: phorvex-staging-demo/demo-api-defense
+ticks: 64
+sync: false
+change:
+In phorvex/internal/decision/ablation.go, add one explanatory comment immediately
+before the existing if base.Ratio &gt; 0 branch, explaining that the positive-ratio
+check avoids division by zero. Change comments only. Preserve executable code.
+
+## Params
+policy: phorvex-staging-demo/demo-api-defense
+ticks: 64
+sync: false
+P-FEA-006: 1.0, 1.2, 1.5</code></pre></div><div class="notice"><strong>Sections are optional; present sections must be valid.</strong>Omit bench to run only parameter experiments, or omit params to run only the code experiment. An empty or malformed section rejects the entire file. Parameters run as separate comparisons with their own controls; they are not applied to the code candidate.</div><div class="command"><div class="command-top"><span>Case B · params-only.md; each parameter ID creates one experiment</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="file-example-params" data-copy-kind="file">Copy file text</button></div><pre><code id="file-example-params"># Parameter comparisons
+
+## Params
+policy: phorvex-staging-demo/demo-api-defense
+ticks: 64
+sync: false
+P-FEA-006: 1.0 1.2 1.5
+P-FEA-005: 300 900 1800</code></pre></div><div class="command"><div class="command-top"><span>Case C · bench-only.txt; a proposed formula change may fail existing tests</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="file-example-bench" data-copy-kind="file">Copy file text</button></div><pre><code id="file-example-bench">[bench]
+policy: phorvex-staging-demo/demo-api-defense
+ticks: 64
+sync: false
+change:
+In phorvex/internal/model/economics.go, change EconomicsMetadata.CostPenalty in
+independent-cost-preferences-v1 mode from
+m.NormPreference.Value*norm + m.FlowPreference.Value*flow to
+1.2*m.NormPreference.Value*norm + m.FlowPreference.Value*flow.
+Preserve legacy and fixed-beta behavior, feasibility gates, SLO checks and budgets.
+Keep the patch minimal.</code></pre></div><p>Put section options before <code>change:</code>. Its description can continue over several lines. <code>sync: false</code> uses cached data; <code>sync: true</code> syncs first. An omitted option uses the bot default. Parameter trial values may be separated by commas or spaces.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">3</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Validate first, or upload to execute</h3><p>Save the example text in a file and attach it as a <strong>document</strong> in the bot chat, rather than pasting the whole file as a text message. To inspect it without running anything, put <code>/validate</code> in the file's <strong>caption</strong>. To run it automatically after validation, send the document with an empty caption.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>What you send</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Valid file, empty caption</td><td>The whole file is parsed and validated, then queued as one batch.</td></tr><tr><td>Valid file, /validate caption</td><td>Structure and parameter domains are checked. No experiment is queued.</td></tr><tr><td>File with one invalid section/value</td><td>Rejected with an error and line number where available. Nothing is queued.</td></tr><tr><td>File containing only bench or only params</td><td>Only the supplied valid section is executed.</td></tr><tr><td>Malformed Word, oversized file, unsupported type</td><td>Rejected; use the supplied examples or export a fresh .docx/.txt.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="notice warn"><strong>Put /validate on the attachment itself.</strong>Sending /validate as a separate message only displays instructions. It does not change the mode of the next upload. Keep section names, option keys, IDs and numeric digits exactly as shown in the examples.</div><p class="small muted">Structural validation checks format, options, parameter IDs and registry value domains. It cannot establish that a proposed code change compiles or passes tests. Each experiment still runs its Go tests and/or runtime replay preflight.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">4</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Follow the batch and collect its results</h3><p>One batch can contain up to five experiments. They execute sequentially on the host. Each experiment returns its usual report files; the batch also returns JSON and CSV summaries with job IDs and statuses. A runtime failure is reported and other independent experiments continue. Use <code>/cancel</code> to cancel the running batch and skip its remaining experiments.</p><div class="notice good"><strong>Progress stays visible while work runs.</strong>A single editable activity card shows the current task, elapsed time and batch experiment number. It refreshes every eight seconds while waiting for a model, tests, builds or replay. The moving bar is an activity indicator, without an invented percentage or ETA. Terminal states include DONE, PARTIAL, NEEDS_DATA, FAILED, CANCELLED and INTERRUPTED.</div><div class="command"><div class="command-top"><span>Illustrative activity card; not a live job</span></div><pre>⠋ BATCH job ab12cd34ef56 — RUNNING
+[□□■■■□□□□□] Experiment 2/3 (bench): running Go unit tests: candidate
+Elapsed: 3m 10s | Current task: 42s
+Activity indicator; no completion-time estimate.</pre></div><p class="small muted">PARTIAL in a batch summary means some experiments succeeded and others failed. PARTIAL in an individual experiment report refers to incomplete data coverage. Read these statuses separately.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="notice"><strong>Caption spelling matters.</strong>Use <code>/validate</code> with a forward slash. An unknown caption, including <code>\validate</code>, is rejected without downloading or queuing. Use an empty caption to run.</div><h3 class="spaced">Alternative: a command-list file</h3><p>Use one complete command per line. For multiline descriptions, use the section format above.</p><div class="command"><div class="command-top"><span>Case D · command-list.txt</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="file-example-commands" data-copy-kind="file">Copy file text</button></div><pre><code id="file-example-commands">/param P-FEA-006 1.0 1.2 1.5 policy=phorvex-staging-demo/demo-api-defense ticks=64 nosync
+/bench policy=phorvex-staging-demo/demo-api-defense ticks=64 nosync In phorvex/internal/decision/ablation.go, add one explanatory comment immediately before the existing if base.Ratio &gt; 0 branch, explaining that the positive-ratio check avoids division by zero. Change comments only. Preserve all executable code and behavior.</code></pre></div><p class="small muted">Example rejection: <code>P-FEA-006: 0</code> is outside the allowed domain; <code>ticks: 5001</code> is invalid; <code>[bench]</code> without a change is incomplete. Fix every reported issue before resubmitting.</p>
+      <div class="notice">A study file is one experiment containing comparison arms. Legacy sections remain independent experiments; a History section does not stack on a previous Cost candidate. Study PARTIAL can indicate missing inputs or selector fallback.</div></section>
+
+      <section class="chapter" id="study" aria-labelledby="study-title">
+<div class="chapter-head"><span class="number">07</span><div><h2 id="study-title">Dynamic Cost and History benchmarks</h2><p>One JSON file: configured comparison arms, recorded seeds and shared reports, without code generation.</p></div></div>
+<div class="notice good"><strong>This workflow is deployed.</strong>A study executes registered reference algorithms using the supplied configuration. Change arms, selector settings and seeds without asking a model for another patch. A new, unregistered algorithm still requires implementation.</div>
+<h3>1. Choose one of the three ready-made files</h3><p>Send <code>/templates</code>. Start with a synthetic example to learn the workflow. <code>/study</code> displays help; attaching the JSON file starts an experiment.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Comparison</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>study-cost-synthetic.json</code></td><td>Legacy versus endpoint-v1 cost, the same deterministic B=1 selector, three seeds</td><td>Synthetic; 6 arm/seed runs</td></tr><tr><td><code>study-history-synthetic.json</code></td><td>Deterministic, entropy, score-only stochastic and history-weighted selectors; three seeds</td><td>Synthetic; 12 arm/seed runs</td></tr><tr><td><code>study-history-recorded.json</code></td><td>The same four selectors on a prepared recorded dataset</td><td>Recorded; requires a valid trace and ledgers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="notice warn"><strong>A synthetic example is not a production outcome.</strong>The two synthetic files demonstrate computation and history behavior. The recorded request returns <code>NEEDS_DATA</code> until the required evidence is installed. Existing legacy snapshots or an all-PASS <code>/check</code> do not establish that these ledgers are available.</div>
+<h3 class="spaced">2. Configure and validate the request</h3><p>Download a file from the builder below, or edit its JSON. Preserve the keys and identifiers. Attach it with <code>/validate</code> as the caption; after PASS, attach the same file with an empty caption to run.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>arms</code></td><td>1–8 named arms. The first arm is the decision-comparison reference; all arms share the input trace.</td></tr><tr><td><code>seeds</code></td><td>1–16 distinct integers. With identical input, configuration and implementation, draws are reproducible.</td></tr><tr><td><code>epsilon</code></td><td>Maximum expected score loss, not a guarantee for each draw. Zero permits only maximal-score choices.</td></tr><tr><td><code>history.window_seconds</code></td><td>Counter lookback window; it does not alter the mandatory 24-hour coverage contract.</td></tr><tr><td><code>history.mode</code></td><td><code>observed</code> consumes recorded events. <code>simulated-starts</code> uses explicit synthetic acknowledgements and requires synthetic evidence.</td></tr><tr><td><code>trace</code> or <code>dataset</code></td><td>Supply exactly one: inline trace data, or <code>"dataset":"recorded"</code> for a prepared server dataset for this policy.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Limits: 2 MiB per JSON file, up to 5,000 ticks and 100,000 candidate rows across all arms and seeds. Do not wrap JSON in <code>[bench]</code>. Legacy <code>ticks</code> and <code>sync</code> are not top-level study fields.</p>
+<h3 class="spaced">3. Choose the cost model and selector</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>cost.kind: provided</code></td><td>Consume authoritative c and rho inputs; useful for isolating selectors with a fixed cost estimator.</td></tr><tr><td><code>cost.kind: legacy</code></td><td>Combine supplied norm/flow terms with explicit weights and a chi gate. Values are dimensionless.</td></tr><tr><td><code>cost.kind: endpoint-v1</code></td><td>Duration estimates and endpoint-second reservations, including cold/censored data and quarantine-specific accounting.</td></tr><tr><td><code>selector.kind</code></td><td><code>deterministic</code>, <code>entropy</code> or <code>history</code>. The History example includes a score-only arm with history weights set to zero.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For a History comparison, hold cost and gates fixed across selectors. For a Cost comparison, hold selector and move count fixed. This workflow uses B=1. Example parameters are study choices, not approved production defaults.</p>
+<h3 class="spaced">4. Supply meaningful history and recorded evidence</h3>
+<ul><li><code>F</code> counts actual optional opportunities after gates; a retry is not another opportunity.</li><li><code>S</code> counts acknowledged execution starts. A draw, failed dispatch or proposed choice does not increment S.</li><li>Security effects require matching verified events. An apparently successful action does not automatically earn verified coverage.</li><li>Each arm/seed has isolated history. In observed mode, counterfactual choices do not generate actual execution events.</li><li>Missing required history triggers deterministic <code>PICKER_UNAVAILABLE</code> fallback and <code>PARTIAL</code>. Missing cost accounting is not initialized to unused budget.</li></ul>
+<p>For recorded mode, the data owner must prepare and import chronological identities, gate verdicts, budgets, duration samples and valid history events. Renaming synthetic data does not make it recorded evidence. This reference runner consumes the required gate/scheduler verdicts; it does not rerun production schedulers or actuators.</p>
+<h3 class="spaced">Complete recorded-data request</h3><p>This is a structurally complete file. Without the prepared dataset, its correct outcome is <code>NEEDS_DATA</code>.</p><div class="command"><div class="command-top"><span>study-history-recorded.json</span><button class="copy" type="button" data-copy="study-recorded-example" data-copy-kind="file">Copy file text</button></div><pre><code id="study-recorded-example">{
+  &quot;schema&quot;: &quot;phorvex-bench.study.v1&quot;,
+  &quot;title&quot;: &quot;History v1.1 on normalized recorded evidence&quot;,
+  &quot;policy&quot;: &quot;phorvex-staging-demo/demo-api-defense&quot;,
+  &quot;dataset&quot;: &quot;recorded&quot;,
+  &quot;seeds&quot;: [7, 19, 41],
+  &quot;history&quot;: {&quot;mode&quot;: &quot;observed&quot;, &quot;window_seconds&quot;: 3600},
+  &quot;arms&quot;: [
+    {&quot;id&quot;: &quot;deterministic&quot;, &quot;cost&quot;: {&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;endpoint-v1&quot;}, &quot;selector&quot;: {&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;deterministic&quot;}},
+    {&quot;id&quot;: &quot;entropy&quot;, &quot;cost&quot;: {&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;endpoint-v1&quot;}, &quot;selector&quot;: {&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;entropy&quot;, &quot;tau&quot;: 1, &quot;epsilon&quot;: 0.5}},
+    {&quot;id&quot;: &quot;score-only&quot;, &quot;cost&quot;: {&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;endpoint-v1&quot;}, &quot;selector&quot;: {&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;history&quot;, &quot;epsilon&quot;: 0.5, &quot;score_scale&quot;: 1, &quot;eta_pair&quot;: 0, &quot;eta_action&quot;: 0, &quot;eta_fact&quot;: 0, &quot;kappa_pair&quot;: 1, &quot;kappa_action&quot;: 1, &quot;q_ref&quot;: 1, &quot;x0&quot;: 0.7, &quot;a0&quot;: 0}},
+    {&quot;id&quot;: &quot;history&quot;, &quot;cost&quot;: {&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;endpoint-v1&quot;}, &quot;selector&quot;: {&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;history&quot;, &quot;epsilon&quot;: 0.5, &quot;score_scale&quot;: 1, &quot;eta_pair&quot;: 2, &quot;eta_action&quot;: 1, &quot;eta_fact&quot;: 0, &quot;kappa_pair&quot;: 1, &quot;kappa_action&quot;: 1, &quot;q_ref&quot;: 1, &quot;x0&quot;: 0.7, &quot;a0&quot;: 0}}
+  ]
+}</code></pre></div><div class="builder study-builder" id="study-builder"><h3>Build a ready-to-upload JSON file</h3><p class="intro">This builder prepares a file locally. It does not connect to the server or run a benchmark. Edit the downloaded JSON for additional settings.</p><div class="fields">
+<div class="field"><label for="study-template">Experiment template</label><select id="study-template"><option value="cost-synthetic">Cost · synthetic</option><option value="history-synthetic">History · synthetic</option><option value="history-recorded">History · recorded</option></select></div>
+<div class="field"><label for="study-policy">Policy</label><input id="study-policy" type="text" value="phorvex-staging-demo/demo-api-defense" spellcheck="false"></div>
+<div class="field"><label for="study-seeds">Seeds separated by spaces or commas</label><input id="study-seeds" type="text" value="7 19 41" spellcheck="false"></div>
+<div class="field"><label for="study-epsilon">Expected-loss budget, epsilon</label><input id="study-epsilon" type="number" value="0.5" min="0" max="1000000000000" step="any"></div>
+<div class="field"><label for="study-window">History window, seconds</label><input id="study-window" type="number" value="3600" min="1" max="31536000" step="any"></div></div>
+<p id="study-status" class="builder-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p><p><button class="btn primary" type="button" id="study-download">Download JSON file</button></p>
+<details class="study-preview"><summary>File preview</summary><pre><code id="study-output"></code></pre></details></div>
+<h3 id="endpoint-v3">V2/V3 endpoint cost and adaptive lambda</h3>
+<p>For this specific comparison, use <code>endpoint-v3-synthetic.json</code> to run 64 labelled synthetic ticks, or <code>endpoint-v3-recorded.json</code> to request qualified recorded inputs. Synthetic arms run in the shared Go reference kernel. Qualified recorded arms run through the reviewed brain study engine on actual stage snapshots, with exact baseline validation. No candidate is deployed by the request.</p>
+<p>Send the JSON as a document. Use <code>/validate</code> in its caption for structure validation, then send it without a caption to execute. The result includes HTML, candidate CSV, full JSON and an evidence ZIP. V3 state follows recorded timestamps; costs and measured spending are never derived from hypothetical selected actions.</p>
+<div class="notice warn"><strong>BLOCKED means required evidence is missing.</strong>Old snapshots lack move-attributed endpoint history and a complete candidate export for the new cost gate. The bot lists these inputs before model/build work; it cannot reconstruct missing history. A synthetic COMPLETE result verifies the reference calculation, not production readiness. The original reviewed V3 TXT also routes to this check; arbitrary edits return it to the code-benchmark workflow.</div>
+<div class="screen-only"><button class="btn" type="button" data-endpoint-template="synthetic">Download synthetic V2/V3 JSON</button> <button class="btn" type="button" data-endpoint-template="recorded">Download recorded V2/V3 request</button></div>
+<p>Customize <code>study.slo_target</code>, <code>study.window</code> (W, seconds), <code>study.worth_threshold</code> and <code>config.lookback</code> (L, seconds) in the request. The bot changes a separate study copy with <code>accepted=false</code> and <code>study_assumption=true</code>; facts, gates, UIDs, move intervals, duration samples and ledger authority stay unchanged. Both W and L must fit genuine continuous history before every selected tick. Expanded windows also require the normalizer's complete retained-history contract. Insufficient history returns BLOCKED with requested and available seconds before either arm runs.</p>
+<p>The short template requests W=600, L=600 and s=0.9 for <code>phorvex-staging-demo/stage-bench-defense</code>. The original template requests W=2,592,000 (30 days), L=86,400 (one day) and s=0.999; it requires continuous retained history and verified capacity to store and process it within the allowed time. The 30-day capability is not yet validated. A successful short study does not satisfy the original 30-day requirement. <code>dataset-manifest.json</code> preserves original normalization; <code>study-settings.json</code> records requested and effective settings.</p>
+<p><strong>Fresh stage generations.</strong> The configured periodic stage sync and endpoint refresh keep collecting current source/image/UID-matched data. Each recorded request freezes one qualified generation; subsequent requests can use a newer generation. Refresh preserves the last good manifest if history is insufficient or the source changes, and records its current reason in <code>mathlab/endpoint-refresh/NAMESPACE/POLICY.status.json</code>. Results retain their actual interval and source, so an older retained generation is never relabelled as fresh. The 30-day W / one-day L request needs all of that continuous retained history plus verified storage/runtime readiness; the 30-day capability is not yet validated.</p>
+<p><code>budget.acting_admission = BLOCKED: offline experiment</code> is expected: replay does not authorize physical actions. Judge execution by the result’s top-level <code>state</code>, <code>comparable_ticks</code> and missing reasons; this admission label alone is not a failed comparison.</p>
+<div class="screen-only"><button class="btn" type="button" data-endpoint-template="stage-600s">Download 600-second stage request</button> <button class="btn" type="button" data-endpoint-template="original-30d-pending">Download original 30-day request (readiness pending)</button></div>
+
+<p class="small">These templates are also available through <code>/templates</code>. Inspect <code>evidence</code>, <code>comparable_ticks</code>, both arm results, lambda diagnostics and missing reasons. Unavailable actual charges and verified effects stay null.</p>
+</section>
