+      <section class="chapter" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-title">
+        <div class="chapter-head"><span class="number">09</span><div><h2 id="results-title">How to read the report</h2><p>Establish comparison validity, examine metric changes, then assess your hypothesis.</p></div></div><h3>Study reports: read evidence and status first</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>COMPLETE</code></td><td>The reference experiment completed with adequate inputs; this is not algorithm approval or a measured live outcome.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PARTIAL</code></td><td>Some ticks lack inputs or a selector fell back to deterministic behavior. Inspect gap and fallback counts.</td></tr><tr><td><code>NEEDS_DATA</code></td><td>The dataset or required evidence is unavailable. Supply the missing data.</td></tr><tr><td><code>CANCELLED</code></td><td>Cancellation was applied; partial evidence may be retained.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>File / metric</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>study-&lt;job&gt;-report.html</code></td><td>Arm/seed summary, evidence type, data gaps and paired decisions.</td></tr><tr><td><code>study-&lt;job&gt;-metrics.csv</code></td><td>Usable ticks, fallback counts, selections and expected score loss.</td></tr><tr><td><code>study-&lt;job&gt;-result.json</code></td><td>Machine-readable status, evidence gaps and available/unavailable metrics.</td></tr><tr><td><code>study-&lt;job&gt;-evidence.zip</code></td><td>Frozen input/manifest, candidates.csv, ticks.csv and per-candidate probabilities.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="notice warn">More changed decisions do not mean improvement. Do not calculate a cost reduction between dimensionless legacy values and endpoint-seconds. Null/unavailable is not zero. Counterfactual verified_coverage and realized_cost are not observed; choices or summed reservations do not establish actual coverage or spend.</div><h3>Below: replay reports for /bench and /param</h3>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">1</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Check status and actual data coverage</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Report field</th><th>Meaning and next step</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>COMPLETE</code></td><td>The comparison finished with the expected coverage. This label alone does not establish improvement.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PARTIAL</code></td><td>Some data was ineligible or could not be compared. Read the denominators and exclusion reasons; do not treat this as equivalent to full coverage.</td></tr><tr><td><code>FAILED / INVALID</code></td><td>The result is not valid evidence for accepting the candidate. Resolve the reported problem first.</td></tr><tr><td><code>64/64</code> or similar</td><td>Read actual counts separately for control and each arm. Requesting 64 or 500 ticks does not guarantee that many valid samples.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">2</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Understand the three /bench arms</h3><div class="grid3"><article class="card"><span class="badge">control</span><h4>The deployed replay binary</h4><p>A tick is control-eligible only if this binary exactly reproduces the recorded stage output.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="badge green">base-local</span><h4>The baseline source, built here</h4><p>For a valid comparison, fidelity must be <code>EXACT</code> and its changed-decision share against control must be zero.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="badge amber">candidate</span><h4>The code generated for your request</h4><p>This is a different brain revision. Its results are labeled <code>OBSERVATIONAL</code>.</p></article></div><div class="notice danger"><strong>If base-local is DIVERGED, do not use the candidate result to make a decision.</strong>First establish why the local baseline differs from the deployed binary. EXACT describes decision reproduction on the inspected ticks; it does not by itself guarantee that both binary files are byte-identical.</div><p class="small muted"><code>/param</code> normally reports <code>MATCHED_REPLAY</code>. <code>OBSERVATIONAL</code> is expected for <code>/bench</code> because it compares different source revisions.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">3</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Read the metrics against your experiment question</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>What it measures</th><th>How to interpret it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>M-P21</code></td><td>Share of ticks whose selected action set differs from control</td><td>Behavioral sensitivity, with no inherent good/bad direction. It is not an improvement score.</td></tr><tr><td><code>M-P26</code></td><td>Selected moves per tick</td><td>A reduction may remove unnecessary moves or omit a needed response.</td></tr><tr><td><code>M-P28</code></td><td>Estimated disruption cost of selected moves</td><td>A cost-model estimate, not measured live disruption or money spent.</td></tr><tr><td><code>M-P19</code></td><td>Share of attack-backed asset-ticks with no selected move</td><td>An increase calls for inspecting no-op reasons and potentially missed responses.</td></tr><tr><td><code>M-P22</code></td><td>Share of attack-backed opportunities with complete evidence and an admitted positive-gain action</td><td>Describes a modeled opportunity, not a successful live defense.</td></tr><tr><td><code>M-P25</code></td><td>Predicted coverage using common control-arm weights</td><td>Useful for comparing modeled coverage; it is not an observed security outcome.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="small muted">Metrics do not all have the same population or denominator. <code>n/a</code> means unavailable, not zero. Read intervals and sample counts alongside the headline values.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="step"><span class="step-index">4</span><div class="step-body"><h3>Separate changed behavior from improvement</h3><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>A wholly hypothetical example for learning how to interpret results. These numbers do not come from a real bot run.</caption><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Control</th><th>Candidate</th><th>What this metric alone says</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Changed decisions <code>M-P21</code></td><td>0%</td><td>25%</td><td>One in four compared ticks has a different selected set.</td></tr><tr><td>Moves per tick <code>M-P26</code></td><td>0.8</td><td>0.6</td><td>Mean selected moves are 25% lower.</td></tr><tr><td>Estimated cost <code>M-P28</code></td><td>0.40</td><td>0.32</td><td>Mean modeled cost is 20% lower.</td></tr><tr><td>No-op under attack <code>M-P19</code></td><td>10%</td><td>15%</td><td>Up 5 percentage points; inspect possible adverse effects.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="notice warn"><strong>This does not establish that “the brain improved by 25%.”</strong>Estimated cost decreased, but no-op under attack also increased. Interpret the metrics together with the hypothesis and safety constraints. A single number cannot determine release acceptance.</div><p class="small muted">In the CSV, <code>ARM_LEVEL</code> is the arm's value and <code>PAIRED_DIFF_VS_CONTROL</code> is a difference over matched pairs. Keep them distinct. <code>M-P18</code> uses each arm's own objective pricing, so comparing raw values after changing the cost formula can be misleading.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="notice good"><strong>A recorded pipeline verification, with no optimization claim</strong>In the recorded check on <b>2026-10-28</b>, job <code>0deacfd4c571</code> completed on baseline <code>0058a3edd2eb</code> with a comment-only candidate: 64/64 ticks, EXACT baseline fidelity, and 398 PASS test events, 2 SKIPs and zero failures per version. Changed decisions were zero. This is historical evidence that the pipeline ran. Use /check and a fresh report to assess the current state.</div>
+      </section>
+
+      <section class="chapter" id="errors" aria-labelledby="errors-title">
+        <div class="chapter-head"><span class="number">10</span><div><h2 id="errors-title">Troubleshooting</h2><p>Keep the job ID, failed stage and error message together.</p></div></div>
+        <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Symptom or message</th><th>Likely meaning</th><th>Next step</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>NEEDS_DATA</code></td><td>Required study evidence is missing.</td><td>Read result.json and data_gaps; prepare the recorded dataset. Repeating the same request does not create evidence.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PICKER_UNAVAILABLE</code></td><td>Required history or fact inputs are incomplete.</td><td>Check lookback coverage and events; do not present fallback choices as a full History result.</td></tr><tr><td><code>Study validation / unknown fields</code></td><td>An input key, type, seed or limit is invalid.</td><td>Start from a JSON template. Duplicate seeds, NaN and top-level ticks/sync are not accepted.</td></tr><tr><td><code>no in-scope diff … model declined</code></td><td>The /bench planner returned no permissible patch; the benchmark has not run.</td><td>Read plan-&lt;job&gt;.json. Use study JSON for supported Cost/History comparisons; use a bounded, precise request for code changes.</td></tr><tr><td>File rejected / section or line error</td><td>The format, key, value or an empty section is invalid.</td><td>Start with /templates, fix every issue, and attach the file with /validate to check again. Do not translate the example keys.</td></tr><tr><td>File validation PASS followed by a failed experiment</td><td>The structure was valid, but runtime preflight, tests or build failed.</td><td>Read that job's report and log. Structural validation does not guarantee passing code or adequate data.</td></tr><tr><td>PARTIAL in a batch summary</td><td>Some experiments succeeded and others failed.</td><td>Read batch JSON/CSV for each job ID and status. This differs from PARTIAL data coverage in an individual report.</td></tr><tr><td>The bot does not reply</td><td>Your account is unauthorized, the invitation is used/expired, or the service is unavailable.</td><td>Try the private chat and ask the owner for a fresh invitation. If you are already authorized, the service needs inspection.</td></tr><tr><td><code>BOT_REPO does not contain the deployed commit</code></td><td>The new binary exists, but its exact source commit is missing from the server clone.</td><td>The operator should fetch that commit into BOT_REPO, then rerun <code>/check</code> and start a new experiment. A tag is not required.</td></tr><tr><td><code>no recorded ticks</code> or revision mismatch</td><td>No matching-revision data is available for the chosen policy.</td><td>Check the policy and run without <code>nosync</code>. If recording is inactive, inspect that policy's capture setup.</td></tr><tr><td>Free disk below the threshold</td><td>The <code>/bench</code> start guard refused the job.</td><td>Free space on the server, then run <code>/check</code>. Lowering the guard alone does not solve a space shortage.</td></tr><tr><td><code>MODE_CONFLICT</code></td><td>The parameter conflicts with the current economics mode, such as beta with costPreferences.</td><td>Choose a compatible parameter. Do not change the live economics mode just to force this experiment.</td></tr><tr><td>Invalid value / unsupported parameter</td><td>The ID, domain, unit, or override support is wrong.</td><td>Use ASCII numbers, the right unit and a supported parameter. Read the preflight message.</td></tr><tr><td><code>LLM step failed</code> or refused patch</td><td>There is a model-response, context, or file-scope problem.</td><td>Supply an exact file/function and a smaller change. Remove out-of-scope edits. Authentication/quota errors need service-configuration inspection.</td></tr><tr><td><code>base-local Go tests FAILED</code></td><td>Even the baseline failed tests on the server.</td><td>Inspect the baseline log. Code, tooling or resource pressure may be responsible. Do not attribute this directly to the candidate formula.</td></tr><tr><td><code>candidate Go tests FAILED</code></td><td>The candidate violated an existing test.</td><td>Read the log. The change may be wrong, or the intended contract may need independent code and test review. Do not bypass tests to obtain numbers.</td></tr><tr><td><code>build failed</code></td><td>Compilation, toolchain, module or resource problem</td><td>Inspect the failed build stage and log. There is no valid benchmark result yet.</td></tr><tr><td><code>DIVERGED</code></td><td>The rebuilt baseline does not reproduce control decisions.</td><td>Stop interpreting candidate comparisons and resolve baseline fidelity first.</td></tr><tr><td><code>n/a</code> or incomplete coverage</td><td>Required evidence, a valid denominator or replayable samples are missing.</td><td>Read data_status, reason and sample counts. Do not record <code>n/a</code> as zero.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="notice"><strong>For troubleshooting, keep these items:</strong>Job ID, the submitted command, the failed stage, and that job's log or report. Resolve the first failure before queuing several similar expensive experiments.</div>
+      </section>
